1
00:00:15,008 --> 00:00:17,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, let's start with this one question.

2
00:00:17,169 --> 00:00:17,970
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quite common.

3
00:00:19,310 --> 00:00:21,571
[SPEAKER_00]: How to make sati continuous?

4
00:00:24,313 --> 00:00:29,035
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, how to be mindful of the right thing continuously.

5
00:00:29,756 --> 00:00:30,796
[SPEAKER_00]: You were speaking of it yesterday.

6
00:00:30,816 --> 00:00:31,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

7
00:00:31,496 --> 00:00:34,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, simple answer is by understanding what sati is.

8
00:00:37,637 --> 00:00:47,125
[SPEAKER_01]: By abandoning the notion that sati pertains to that momentary-momentary, moment-to-moment awareness or something like that, that's simply a contradiction.

9
00:00:47,286 --> 00:00:48,487
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't... it can't exist like that.

10
00:00:49,147 --> 00:00:54,952
[SPEAKER_00]: But what I'm interested in is the sati which is in regard to freedom from suffering.

11
00:00:55,012 --> 00:00:55,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

12
00:00:55,793 --> 00:00:56,313
[SPEAKER_00]: The relevant.

13
00:00:56,674 --> 00:00:58,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Not sati to make my life better.

14
00:00:59,016 --> 00:00:59,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

15
00:00:59,476 --> 00:01:03,740
[SPEAKER_00]: To make me more productive, or smarter, more intelligent, more focused.

16
00:01:04,587 --> 00:01:11,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not even... not necessarily just that, but even like... even people who are into meditation techniques and so on.

17
00:01:11,651 --> 00:01:13,072
[SPEAKER_01]: The idea of sati is that.

18
00:01:13,632 --> 00:01:18,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Observing from moment to a moment to a moment to a moment, and then you have the uninterrupted mindfulness.

19
00:01:19,175 --> 00:01:24,338
[SPEAKER_01]: But mindfulness is not structured moment to moment.

20
00:01:24,418 --> 00:01:32,702
[SPEAKER_01]: It's structured in a... in a... in a different... differently... different hierarchy of more general things, more general intentions, more general...

21
00:01:35,053 --> 00:01:47,182
[SPEAKER_01]: States of mind, basically, whereby if you get established upon them, then the particular things, no matter how fast, how fleeting they're occurring, your mindfulness is not affected by it.

22
00:01:47,742 --> 00:01:56,028
[SPEAKER_01]: But if your mindfulness is established upon particular, fleeting things, then your mindfulness will be flying away with them as they cease.

23
00:01:58,850 --> 00:02:02,132
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the relevant manner, basically, yeah,

24
00:02:05,053 --> 00:02:10,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Establishing your mind at a more general phenomenon.

25
00:02:10,836 --> 00:02:20,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, and it can be various things in terms of establishing mindfulness upon them, so you don't have to... it's not like fixed, you must establish upon this.

26
00:02:20,781 --> 00:02:34,207
[SPEAKER_01]: You can use your body, you can use your feelings, you can use your thoughts, you can use the motivations behind your actions, you can do all those things to establish your mind in regard to things you're doing on a particular level, and that's already mindfulness.

27
00:02:36,033 --> 00:02:49,796
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of, like, if you wanted the most direct answer how to be mindful that's relevant to the suffering and freedom from it, well, you have to be mindful of your feelings or of the present feeling, whichever that is.

28
00:02:51,077 --> 00:02:58,839
[SPEAKER_01]: And even with the feelings, you can see even they have this kind of hierarchy of more general and more ambiguous and so on.

29
00:02:59,519 --> 00:03:04,580
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, because even the most kind of vague

30
00:03:05,647 --> 00:03:13,849
[SPEAKER_01]: The five-elven mood is basically... you can regard it almost as a feeling, because it has a certain feel to it.

31
00:03:15,189 --> 00:03:23,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you might not be able to define what you perceive in terms of that mood as a phenomenon, but you can certainly say, yeah, it feels a bit... it's a bit heavy.

32
00:03:23,971 --> 00:03:28,152
[SPEAKER_01]: So, okay, you're perceiving heaviness, but all of those things you perceive, how they feel?

33
00:03:28,512 --> 00:03:29,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Eh... it's a bit unpleasant.

34
00:03:29,753 --> 00:03:30,673
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a bad mood.

35
00:03:32,172 --> 00:03:45,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you can have the most obvious, oh, this is a very pleasurable feeling, that's coming as a result of me having disagree... having agreeable perceptions, such as, eh... nice taste, beautiful sights, and so on.

36
00:03:48,427 --> 00:03:58,456
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you... if you're obviously... if you're only concerned with those things, then the... when those things change, when those things move too quickly, your mindfulness will not remain established, because it was... it was established upon them.

37
00:03:59,938 --> 00:04:11,407
[SPEAKER_01]: But say you establish your mindfulness upon the general mood behind these particular things that are arising and sort of interacting with you.

38
00:04:11,948 --> 00:04:21,796
[SPEAKER_01]: If you establish your mind upon those more general sense of, you know, yeah, my mind feels light and expanded, which is agreeable, which is pleasant.

39
00:04:23,698 --> 00:04:26,680
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can then establish your mind upon that level of feeling.

40
00:04:27,603 --> 00:04:28,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather than the feeling on that level.

41
00:04:29,464 --> 00:04:41,146
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when you do have disagreeable perceptions, they will not induce painful feelings, because your mind is established on the higher feelings being higher than those.

42
00:04:41,206 --> 00:04:43,047
[SPEAKER_01]: And those are like in the suttas, similar.

43
00:04:43,067 --> 00:04:51,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Establish your mind upon the perception of forest, so then, disagreeable perception of the village, when you have to be in the village, will not bother you.

44
00:04:53,069 --> 00:04:54,210
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's for that very reason.

45
00:04:56,419 --> 00:05:04,485
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to establish your mind on a higher feeling... A more general feeling, yeah.

46
00:05:04,565 --> 00:05:12,371
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're saying, if I have an unpleasant feeling, to go to a higher feeling... You could, yeah.

47
00:05:12,451 --> 00:05:19,416
[SPEAKER_01]: If a particular, well, no, if a particular disagreeable feeling bothers you, yeah, you go on a higher feeling.

48
00:05:19,917 --> 00:05:25,101
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, the ultimate higher feeling is accepting that you will die.

49
00:05:26,645 --> 00:05:27,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Not being afraid of that.

50
00:05:28,366 --> 00:05:37,630
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, okay, so whatever has arisen, whatever arises, it will have to cease, because it's not gonna stay and beset me forever.

51
00:05:37,790 --> 00:05:39,271
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just impossible, inconceivable.

52
00:05:39,291 --> 00:05:50,576
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you don't even have to think that anymore, when you've thought it sufficient number of times, because then you just know that whatever has arisen, has to cease, for the very reason that it has arisen.

53
00:05:51,456 --> 00:05:52,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else, nothing other.

54
00:05:53,507 --> 00:05:56,448
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the whole idea of the... and that's why people fear pain.

55
00:05:56,929 --> 00:05:58,549
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why everybody fears pain.

56
00:05:58,689 --> 00:06:05,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, when it arises, there is implicit assumption that's eternal.

57
00:06:05,752 --> 00:06:06,773
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to last forever.

58
00:06:08,174 --> 00:06:22,740
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just have that kind of inside... contains that inside fear, that this pain, that I know... that I don't know how to escape from,

59
00:06:23,420 --> 00:06:25,321
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to sort of affect me forever.

60
00:06:25,742 --> 00:06:28,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Although your reason can tell you, no, nothing lasts forever and so on.

61
00:06:29,084 --> 00:06:30,625
[SPEAKER_01]: On that emotional level, you're locked.

62
00:06:30,745 --> 00:06:33,086
[SPEAKER_01]: You're locked in the assumption forever.

63
00:06:34,847 --> 00:06:43,333
[SPEAKER_01]: And the same for pleasure, which is why, for a mind which is not developed, yeah, pleasure is overly intoxicating.

64
00:06:45,034 --> 00:06:51,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Like to the point of oblivion and genuinely developing views that, yeah, you will endure and last forever.

65
00:06:53,107 --> 00:07:04,016
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start undermining that assumption, then, when the pain arises, you won't feel it, because you know that it's inconceivable for anything to last forever in that sense, in that emotional oppression sense.

66
00:07:04,056 --> 00:07:20,129
[SPEAKER_01]: And in the same sense, your thirst for essential pleasures or pleasures that you can gain in regard to the world and so on, well, they will also fade gradually, because you realize, oh, that's not lasting forever, so why do I even bother?

67
00:07:21,021 --> 00:07:22,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I even expose myself?

68
00:07:22,342 --> 00:07:26,364
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just such a triumphal sort of satisfaction in regard to it.

69
00:07:34,729 --> 00:07:46,235
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the mindfulness, establishing of mindfulness, is basically establishing your mind on a more general level in regard to your present experience here as a whole.

70
00:07:51,153 --> 00:07:56,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it's not, in regard to the present experience, then it just becomes an an abstraction, whatever you're thinking.

71
00:07:57,456 --> 00:08:00,097
[SPEAKER_01]: It just becomes divorced from what you're actually doing.

72
00:08:00,617 --> 00:08:18,386
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you establish it presently, and you're aware of its presence, so to speak, then then it becomes very real, and obviously very functional establishment, whereby anything lower than that establishment, anything less general,

73
00:08:19,165 --> 00:08:24,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything more particular will not... cannot... probably cannot affect you.

74
00:08:24,691 --> 00:08:32,158
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said you would be... you should be taking this as far as necessary, in order to develop final freedom.

75
00:08:32,859 --> 00:08:38,565
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, in the... in this... in the... in that sutra I mentioned, upon the perceptions of the forest, you bend the perceptions of the village,

76
00:08:39,743 --> 00:08:56,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, he recognizes, okay, so now, when I go into the village, I'm not affected by any perceptions that I see there, agreeable or disagreeable, because my mind is properly, fully established upon the perceptions of the life experience as a whole in the forest.

77
00:08:57,099 --> 00:09:02,624
[SPEAKER_01]: But, he also recognizes, disturbances can still come on account of these perceptions in the forest.

78
00:09:02,664 --> 00:09:05,687
[SPEAKER_01]: If something happens in that forest, I could lose my mindfulness.

79
00:09:06,881 --> 00:09:12,044
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, he takes it step further, then he establishes his mind upon the perceptions of Earth.

80
00:09:13,304 --> 00:09:27,312
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, whether he's in the forest, or even more lower in the village, or even lower than that, in a layman's house, crowded house, then he won't be affected, because his mind is fully set, fully established upon the perception of Earth.

81
00:09:27,712 --> 00:09:30,534
[SPEAKER_01]: So, wherever he is on this Earth, he'll be mindful.

82
00:09:32,715 --> 00:09:33,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Presently.

83
00:09:33,996 --> 00:09:44,020
[SPEAKER_01]: So, theoretically, he could lose that mindfulness of being established upon the perception of Earth if he was to leave the Earth, to fly into space or something.

84
00:09:45,401 --> 00:09:56,806
[SPEAKER_01]: But if somebody is able to establish their mind to such a level of perception of Earth, then they could easily establish it upon the perception of space, upon the perception of those general phenomena that pertain to the mind.

85
00:09:56,826 --> 00:09:58,927
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he would be fine even in space, basically.

86
00:09:58,947 --> 00:09:59,487
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm saying.

87
00:10:06,049 --> 00:10:09,070
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you make that perception of Earth to not be abstract?

88
00:10:09,571 --> 00:10:13,172
[SPEAKER_01]: You make it by discerning it in your present experience, right here, right now.

89
00:10:15,073 --> 00:10:15,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is there.

90
00:10:17,314 --> 00:10:20,255
[SPEAKER_01]: But also, in order to discern it, you need to know what it is.

91
00:10:20,295 --> 00:10:22,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Or at least, you need to have some clue.

92
00:10:22,956 --> 00:10:30,179
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's important to basically learn and inform yourself from the suttas and then ponder and then discern it as a phenomenal present.

93
00:10:30,219 --> 00:10:33,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Not discerning in theory only, because that's not a discernment.

94
00:10:34,021 --> 00:10:34,942
[SPEAKER_01]: That's an abstraction.

95
00:10:36,792 --> 00:10:39,454
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is the present thinking, and there is the abstract thinking.

96
00:10:40,474 --> 00:10:44,957
[SPEAKER_01]: And usually, people can't distinguish the two, and then, they kind of disregard thinking altogether.

97
00:10:45,778 --> 00:10:48,480
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's just a very bad idea.

98
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:52,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you think about solidity... Yeah.

99
00:10:53,403 --> 00:10:59,327
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an image, that kind of image of solidity.

100
00:10:59,607 --> 00:10:59,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

101
00:11:00,508 --> 00:11:03,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Surrounding... A concept of... Concept of solidity.

102
00:11:04,899 --> 00:11:13,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Uh, but when with feelings, that's kind of... No, because feelings don't appear as as as concepts, because there will be a perception.

103
00:11:14,365 --> 00:11:15,406
[SPEAKER_01]: They're being felt.

104
00:11:15,626 --> 00:11:21,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to use the faculty of feeling to feel, basically.

105
00:11:21,470 --> 00:11:24,272
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, uh, I think, again, what was it?

106
00:11:24,752 --> 00:11:26,553
[SPEAKER_01]: My teaching is for the one who feels.

107
00:11:26,573 --> 00:11:27,334
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said.

108
00:11:27,914 --> 00:11:30,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, his teaching is for the one who knows how to feel.

109
00:11:31,911 --> 00:11:35,736
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just sort of being liable to feeling, it's not enough.

110
00:11:35,856 --> 00:11:36,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody is that.

111
00:11:36,597 --> 00:11:41,423
[SPEAKER_01]: They actually have to know, to discern, how to feel, and then you will know the feeling.

112
00:11:43,505 --> 00:11:45,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Because most people think they perceive it.

113
00:11:45,548 --> 00:11:47,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people think, when they feel, they're actually perceiving.

114
00:11:48,554 --> 00:11:48,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

115
00:11:48,914 --> 00:11:54,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when you say feeling, pleasant feeling... Yeah, you're already... See, that's what you're perceiving.

116
00:11:54,259 --> 00:11:56,321
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a concept of pleasant feeling.

117
00:11:56,401 --> 00:11:57,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Some sort of whatever.

118
00:11:57,502 --> 00:12:01,906
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I can designate a pleasant feeling, but that designation is on the level of perception.

119
00:12:03,367 --> 00:12:14,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception of sound, perception of utterance, perception of... even a perception of a mental image of either words, pleasant feeling written, or some pleasant experience.

120
00:12:14,576 --> 00:12:15,978
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, all those perceptions.

121
00:12:16,098 --> 00:12:16,178
[SPEAKER_01]: And

122
00:12:17,107 --> 00:12:18,928
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't not have the perceptions.

123
00:12:19,589 --> 00:12:24,772
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you can't not have feelings, because they are inseparable, perception and feeling.

124
00:12:25,772 --> 00:12:38,600
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you can know is how to feel in regard to that which you perceive, and not think that that which you perceive is the same thing that you're feeling, because then there will be a perception.

125
00:12:40,508 --> 00:12:45,070
[SPEAKER_01]: So, simultaneously, while you're perceiving, you're always simultaneously that feeling on a level of feeling.

126
00:12:45,410 --> 00:12:47,011
[SPEAKER_01]: And those two levels cannot mix.

127
00:12:47,191 --> 00:12:48,431
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot come together.

128
00:12:48,532 --> 00:12:49,772
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot communicate.

129
00:12:50,392 --> 00:12:52,793
[SPEAKER_01]: They're actually, like, blind to each other.

130
00:12:53,514 --> 00:12:55,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Your feelings don't know about your perceptions.

131
00:12:56,815 --> 00:12:58,676
[SPEAKER_01]: No, your perceptions know about your feelings.

132
00:12:59,316 --> 00:13:02,998
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your assumption that it's the same, that it's different, that it's both same and different.

133
00:13:03,018 --> 00:13:05,379
[SPEAKER_01]: It's neither the same nor different in regard to perception and feeling.

134
00:13:05,699 --> 00:13:10,221
[SPEAKER_01]: You mix it all up, and then you assume, and then what you perceive is what you feel, what you feel is what you perceive.

135
00:13:11,970 --> 00:13:14,773
[SPEAKER_01]: But feeling feels, perception perceives, and that's it.

136
00:13:15,714 --> 00:13:19,517
[SPEAKER_01]: And whether that thing is same, or different, or neither, or both, that doesn't matter.

137
00:13:19,597 --> 00:13:20,979
[SPEAKER_00]: But one is more general than the other.

138
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:24,102
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

139
00:13:24,162 --> 00:13:24,702
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

140
00:13:24,982 --> 00:13:28,145
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if you would say one is more general than the other, then they would be comparable.

141
00:13:28,506 --> 00:13:28,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

142
00:13:28,786 --> 00:13:30,808
[SPEAKER_01]: And being comparable is only the form of relationship.

143
00:13:30,848 --> 00:13:33,531
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said, they completely are unaware of each other.

144
00:13:34,171 --> 00:13:34,732
[SPEAKER_01]: And because

145
00:13:35,693 --> 00:13:40,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is completely unaware of perception, and perception is completely unaware of feeling.

146
00:13:40,115 --> 00:13:46,177
[SPEAKER_01]: You can, you can, you can free both of them from assumptions and from the sense of self and everything.

147
00:13:46,797 --> 00:13:57,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if they were a relationship, then they would be the agent in control of them, in regard to them, external to them, internal to them, whichever way.

148
00:13:58,561 --> 00:14:00,822
[SPEAKER_00]: What about this, like, joy?

149
00:14:01,502 --> 00:14:03,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Joy, in one case from meditation,

150
00:14:06,057 --> 00:14:06,357
[SPEAKER_00]: Pity.

151
00:14:06,437 --> 00:14:07,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Pity.

152
00:14:08,558 --> 00:14:14,560
[SPEAKER_00]: So, remember we spoke about to will that, to get that going.

153
00:14:15,921 --> 00:14:16,781
[SPEAKER_00]: And one can do that.

154
00:14:17,781 --> 00:14:18,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

155
00:14:18,601 --> 00:14:19,782
[SPEAKER_00]: One can generate pity.

156
00:14:20,562 --> 00:14:20,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

157
00:14:21,242 --> 00:14:22,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, pity.

158
00:14:22,543 --> 00:14:23,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Pali pity.

159
00:14:23,363 --> 00:14:24,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

160
00:14:24,904 --> 00:14:25,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Joy.

161
00:14:25,444 --> 00:14:26,424
[SPEAKER_00]: One can generate joy.

162
00:14:26,684 --> 00:14:28,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

163
00:14:28,565 --> 00:14:29,565
[SPEAKER_00]: As an act of will.

164
00:14:32,506 --> 00:14:34,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, if you, your mind is

165
00:14:34,587 --> 00:14:35,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Very depressed or something.

166
00:14:35,708 --> 00:14:36,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Scattered.

167
00:14:36,668 --> 00:14:37,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Unrestrained.

168
00:14:37,468 --> 00:14:38,649
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have that much practice.

169
00:14:38,669 --> 00:14:38,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

170
00:14:39,369 --> 00:14:41,090
[SPEAKER_00]: But you could, at some stage, do that.

171
00:14:42,931 --> 00:14:43,551
[SPEAKER_00]: And that feels good.

172
00:14:44,451 --> 00:14:46,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

173
00:14:46,132 --> 00:14:47,873
[SPEAKER_00]: And that way, it seems like I'm in control.

174
00:14:49,073 --> 00:14:49,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

175
00:14:49,654 --> 00:14:50,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Good feeling.

176
00:14:50,294 --> 00:14:50,774
[SPEAKER_01]: See, no.

177
00:14:50,994 --> 00:14:56,017
[SPEAKER_01]: You'd be in... Well, you think you would be in control if you then appropriate that good feeling.

178
00:14:56,857 --> 00:15:01,319
[SPEAKER_01]: That good feeling is just the result of a certain way of attending and intending.

179
00:15:01,379 --> 00:15:01,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

180
00:15:02,279 --> 00:15:03,520
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's absolutely fine.

181
00:15:04,102 --> 00:15:04,642
[SPEAKER_01]: You can do it.

182
00:15:04,702 --> 00:15:07,564
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you shouldn't fear that pleasure.

183
00:15:08,324 --> 00:15:17,209
[SPEAKER_01]: When the Buddha said pleasure in regard to jhānas and so on, he realized, oh, hold on, I don't actually have to... because initially, he was disregarding all of the pleasure.

184
00:15:17,430 --> 00:15:19,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Any pleasant feeling was bad.

185
00:15:19,971 --> 00:15:26,835
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, when he started developing jhānas, he realized, no, actually, these feelings are not underlined by any of those unwholesome things.

186
00:15:26,855 --> 00:15:29,476
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no reason to fear this.

187
00:15:29,957 --> 00:15:31,898
[SPEAKER_01]: On the contrary, I should cultivate this.

188
00:15:32,984 --> 00:15:47,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, it was declared as the ultimate way of basically getting the right view, developing jhānas, and then you'll be a fully developed arahant in both ways, as they say, through full understanding and through full development in regard to your body and meditation.

189
00:15:48,841 --> 00:15:50,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, it's that good.

190
00:15:50,983 --> 00:15:52,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, it can be that good.

191
00:15:53,413 --> 00:16:06,218
[SPEAKER_01]: whereby you can intentionally, with the right view, not without the right view, but with the right view, intentionally cultivate joy, or rapture, or whatever, and it won't be unwholesome.

192
00:16:06,458 --> 00:16:08,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you would know it, if it's unwholesome.

193
00:16:08,258 --> 00:16:12,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're just, it's just an intent, you're just intending it.

194
00:16:12,560 --> 00:16:16,041
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the result of the intention that's occurring, not yours to begin

195
00:16:19,578 --> 00:16:21,359
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

196
00:16:21,439 --> 00:16:22,840
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't pertain to the senses.

197
00:16:23,140 --> 00:16:25,721
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's not unwholesome.

198
00:16:26,281 --> 00:16:32,224
[SPEAKER_01]: However, obviously, you can appropriate that joy, which is why Kutujana can develop jhānas.

199
00:16:32,304 --> 00:16:39,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, he'll abandon lots of sensual cravings as a result of it, because this is much more sublime, much more refined.

200
00:16:39,888 --> 00:16:40,909
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yes, you could.

201
00:16:41,029 --> 00:16:41,549
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

202
00:16:41,569 --> 00:16:41,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Like,

203
00:16:42,486 --> 00:16:49,690
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be... you wouldn't be doing it rightly if you then start appropriating that joy and feeling you are in control of that joy, this joy's mind.

204
00:16:50,471 --> 00:16:51,751
[SPEAKER_01]: But with the right view, that wouldn't happen.

205
00:16:52,332 --> 00:17:00,817
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you would just have the proper practice of developing joy and the right view that sort of sees through any occurrence of lust, aversion, delusion.

206
00:17:01,337 --> 00:17:07,320
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it wouldn't be before long that the mind would be completely free from all the hindrances with that development.

207
00:17:12,494 --> 00:17:22,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there wouldn't be any agitation, there wouldn't be any anxiety, even hardships would feel relatively easy, because the mind gets elevated.

208
00:17:22,182 --> 00:17:24,484
[SPEAKER_01]: You're saying the hindrances won't come up again?

209
00:17:25,005 --> 00:17:26,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I say you become an arahant.

210
00:17:26,666 --> 00:17:29,168
[SPEAKER_01]: With the right view and the jhānas, you'll become an arahant.

211
00:17:29,909 --> 00:17:30,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

212
00:17:44,661 --> 00:17:52,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I don't want to say feeling is always in the background, but... Yeah, I see what you mean, yeah.

213
00:17:52,084 --> 00:17:54,645
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to... You can, you can, you can, well...

214
00:17:55,986 --> 00:18:01,108
[SPEAKER_01]: I think, initially, you probably have to, because that's, that's where you're going to find it, because, uh, the mind is, uh...

215
00:18:02,328 --> 00:18:05,490
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... Too used to, too much established upon perception.

216
00:18:05,690 --> 00:18:13,553
[SPEAKER_01]: And, obviously, mind needs to learn how to feel, how to discern feeling among the, you know, things that it perceives.

217
00:18:14,245 --> 00:18:16,126
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, in that sense, it's in the background.

218
00:18:16,306 --> 00:18:17,627
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't find it where you perceive it.

219
00:18:17,647 --> 00:18:20,528
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll find it simultaneously in regards to that which you perceive.

220
00:18:21,029 --> 00:18:28,133
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, you have to refrain from attending to the feeling as if it's a perception.

221
00:18:28,733 --> 00:18:32,535
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to refrain from attending to the feeling as if you will see it.

222
00:18:33,556 --> 00:18:35,196
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not actually behind because...

223
00:18:35,216 --> 00:18:38,458
[SPEAKER_01]: It's right there all the time in regard to whatever you perceive it to.

224
00:18:39,139 --> 00:18:41,680
[SPEAKER_01]: And, obviously, it has its own

225
00:18:42,877 --> 00:18:45,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, each domain has its own laws, so to speak.

226
00:18:45,979 --> 00:18:47,799
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not as diverse as perception.

227
00:18:47,959 --> 00:18:49,540
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't move as quickly as perception.

228
00:18:50,401 --> 00:18:52,702
[SPEAKER_01]: But, obviously, it's much more subtle than perception.

229
00:18:53,102 --> 00:18:54,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Beyond perception.

230
00:18:54,242 --> 00:18:57,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's beyond in terms of it has nothing to do with it.

231
00:18:57,724 --> 00:18:59,805
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not to the same place, so to speak.

232
00:19:00,205 --> 00:19:03,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I mean, place is already applicable only to perception.

233
00:19:04,087 --> 00:19:05,487
[SPEAKER_01]: It comes as a result of perception.

234
00:19:07,048 --> 00:19:07,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Space and place.

235
00:19:08,248 --> 00:19:09,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is in regard to it.

236
00:19:11,722 --> 00:19:16,125
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just like two dimensions, but you're just used to one.

237
00:19:17,306 --> 00:19:19,267
[SPEAKER_01]: But the other one is constantly in regard to it.

238
00:19:20,268 --> 00:19:24,731
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, with feeling, it sounds like it will be unconditioned.

239
00:19:26,352 --> 00:19:28,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it will be, when you completely let go of it.

240
00:19:32,436 --> 00:19:37,979
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the teaching is for the one who feels, and that's why he teaches only suffering and freedom from it.

241
00:19:38,059 --> 00:19:38,580
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

242
00:19:39,520 --> 00:19:40,661
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole dhamma in there.

243
00:19:43,779 --> 00:19:44,799
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nibbāna is pleasant.

244
00:19:45,059 --> 00:19:46,100
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing felt.

245
00:19:47,560 --> 00:19:49,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, no feeling can touch you.

246
00:19:49,240 --> 00:19:50,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you don't feel.

247
00:19:50,501 --> 00:19:54,422
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't feel anything, and that's the extreme, like, peace.

248
00:19:57,522 --> 00:19:59,503
[SPEAKER_00]: The arahant will always see the distinction.

249
00:20:00,283 --> 00:20:01,083
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he won't see it.

250
00:20:01,323 --> 00:20:01,864
[SPEAKER_00]: He'll feel it.

251
00:20:02,024 --> 00:20:02,924
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he feels it.

252
00:20:03,064 --> 00:20:03,624
[SPEAKER_01]: He'll feel.

253
00:20:03,904 --> 00:20:07,385
[SPEAKER_01]: He'll feel, then feeling, and he'll see it from the point of view of perception.

254
00:20:09,245 --> 00:20:14,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, he won't try to see his feeling, nor will he try to feel his perceptions.

255
00:20:16,187 --> 00:20:17,448
[SPEAKER_01]: He'll feel, he'll perceive.

256
00:20:19,568 --> 00:20:22,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, feeling will feel, perception will perceive.

257
00:20:22,649 --> 00:20:23,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentions will intend.

258
00:20:23,710 --> 00:20:27,311
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can attend to a feeling, isn't it?

259
00:20:28,491 --> 00:20:33,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, but see, in order to attend to a feeling, you have to, well, you have to use the proper attention.

260
00:20:34,497 --> 00:20:42,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't know how to attend to the feeling, well, you attend by the means that you're used to, and those are the means of perception, which means you won't be attending to the feeling.

261
00:20:44,121 --> 00:20:46,482
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll still feel it, because feeling is an aggregate.

262
00:20:46,502 --> 00:20:48,003
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't not feel it.

263
00:20:48,263 --> 00:20:51,204
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just you won't be able to discern what feeling is.

264
00:20:51,384 --> 00:20:55,706
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be able to know the feeling until you learn how to feel.

265
00:20:57,446 --> 00:21:00,308
[SPEAKER_01]: So, feeling in itself is not enough for you to learn how to feel.

266
00:21:01,628 --> 00:21:03,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling just affects you, just hits you.

267
00:21:04,197 --> 00:21:05,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Good and bad are neutral.

268
00:21:07,619 --> 00:21:11,280
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by learning how to feel, then, you can eventually transcend it.

269
00:21:11,780 --> 00:21:14,662
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole domain of feeling and all other domains, obviously.

270
00:21:17,263 --> 00:21:19,924
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how to pay attention?

271
00:21:19,944 --> 00:21:22,245
[SPEAKER_01]: By learning how to feel.

272
00:21:22,625 --> 00:21:27,187
[SPEAKER_01]: By recognizing that at any given time of your experience as a whole, the feeling is present there.

273
00:21:28,948 --> 00:21:31,369
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I ask you now how you... And that's the thing.

274
00:21:31,449 --> 00:21:33,270
[SPEAKER_01]: You can discern how to feel because there is

275
00:21:35,456 --> 00:21:41,159
[SPEAKER_01]: An implicit knowledge of the feeling, or a degree of recognition.

276
00:21:41,659 --> 00:21:44,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe it's not to the level of knowledge, but certainly...

277
00:21:44,901 --> 00:21:46,082
[SPEAKER_00]: If you ask me, how do I feel?

278
00:21:46,142 --> 00:21:47,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if I ask you, how do you feel now?

279
00:21:47,783 --> 00:21:48,943
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be able to answer.

280
00:21:49,484 --> 00:21:50,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you say, well, I don't quite know.

281
00:21:50,944 --> 00:21:58,809
[SPEAKER_01]: It means, okay, so you feel neither like this, nor like that, which means you feel kind of neutral, veering towards unpleasant, because you don't really know it.

282
00:21:59,869 --> 00:22:02,911
[SPEAKER_01]: So, bored or whatever, you know.

283
00:22:03,611 --> 00:22:04,852
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, I've already answered.

284
00:22:05,112 --> 00:22:08,794
[SPEAKER_00]: I've given some sort of... these words to describe that.

285
00:22:08,814 --> 00:22:09,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

286
00:22:09,574 --> 00:22:09,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

287
00:22:10,054 --> 00:22:13,316
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't still necessarily mean that you have confused the two.

288
00:22:14,037 --> 00:22:23,982
[SPEAKER_01]: That you've started... Or rather, you can describe the feeling in terms of designation, in terms of perception, but you won't assume that that perception stands for that which you feel at the time when you're describing it.

289
00:22:31,046 --> 00:22:33,367
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you can be mindful of the feeling

290
00:22:34,302 --> 00:22:48,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Or feelings, as they arise respectively, at any time, at any place, in any situation, because they're not directly linked to the actual, like, particular sort of perceptions, and so on.

291
00:22:48,851 --> 00:22:50,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, they arise as a whole.

292
00:22:50,672 --> 00:22:54,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception, feeling, and intention arise together, cease together.

293
00:22:55,996 --> 00:23:02,700
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when there is a disagreeable perception, or whatever, that has arisen,

294
00:23:03,206 --> 00:23:08,891
[SPEAKER_01]: For an untrained mind, there is most likely going to be unpleasant feeling as well.

295
00:23:09,491 --> 00:23:11,193
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't necessarily have to be the case.

296
00:23:11,333 --> 00:23:16,997
[SPEAKER_01]: And because it doesn't have to be the case, freedom from disagreeable perceptions in terms of suffering is possible.

297
00:23:20,620 --> 00:23:24,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if perception perceives and feeling feels, it has nothing to do with you.

298
00:23:24,263 --> 00:23:26,305
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a feeling from disagreeable perceptions.

299
00:23:27,426 --> 00:23:32,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Disagreeable perceptions for Putujan means suffering, means feeling pain, feeling unpleasant.

300
00:23:34,162 --> 00:23:44,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that mix, or rather that mixing up, that wrong assumption in regard to perception and feeling, feeling, perception, the whole thing, it's so hardened that now it's a solid thing.

301
00:23:45,305 --> 00:23:47,506
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for him, disagreeable means pain.

302
00:23:48,826 --> 00:23:53,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Disagreeable perceptions, discomfort, any of that kind, means emotional pain.

303
00:23:56,528 --> 00:24:02,230
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he needs to kind of unravel the whole thing, the whole assumption, and then

304
00:24:03,690 --> 00:24:10,852
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you can perceive, or rather, if your experience can let the perceptions perceive and feelings feel, you become redundant.

305
00:24:10,932 --> 00:24:11,612
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's jhāna.

306
00:24:31,040 --> 00:24:33,782
[SPEAKER_01]: My sense of self becomes redundant and disappears.

307
00:24:34,543 --> 00:24:43,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Temporarily, based on the intention of that establishment, upon that establishment, but with the right view, that's all it takes.

308
00:24:45,051 --> 00:24:51,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, the view of the self will disappear, the views of the conceit will disappear, and so on, and then, yeah, you will be not around.

309
00:24:52,937 --> 00:24:55,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Because perception perceives, feeling feels.

310
00:24:55,539 --> 00:24:56,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Intention intends.

311
00:24:59,033 --> 00:25:11,557
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this sense itself now, that lingers in regard and kind of hovers over these things, you realize, well, that's just another perception, isn't it, that perceives this feeling in regard to it, that feels an intention in regard to it, that intends.

312
00:25:12,717 --> 00:25:23,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, the subtler sense of I am in regard to that, oh, well, the fact that I was just able to designate it means that's another perception that perceives, that has a feeling in regard to it, that feels an intention, that intends.

313
00:25:23,930 --> 00:25:34,236
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can push this infinitely until you get tired and you realize, oh, this whole idea, this whole notion, this whole concept of I am lingering, is just a more subtle perception in regard to the arisen perception.

314
00:25:34,296 --> 00:25:36,278
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a more subtle feeling in regard to the arisen feeling.

315
00:25:36,618 --> 00:25:39,079
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a more subtle intention in regard to the arisen intention.

316
00:25:39,540 --> 00:25:42,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, all you're going to have is feeling, perception, intention.

317
00:25:44,483 --> 00:25:51,567
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you kind of drill that long enough, any notion of me and I am,

318
00:25:52,023 --> 00:25:56,988
[SPEAKER_01]: It just fades, evaporates, like heating a pan that had water in it.

319
00:25:58,250 --> 00:26:05,417
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, if there's a lot of water, you might have to heat it longer, but eventually everything will evaporate and then you can stop heating it.

320
00:26:40,552 --> 00:26:41,853
[SPEAKER_01]: By knowing what jhāna is?

321
00:26:44,715 --> 00:26:46,557
[SPEAKER_01]: By not mixing your feelings with your perceptions?

322
00:26:47,177 --> 00:26:48,158
[SPEAKER_01]: By learning how to feel?

323
00:26:48,819 --> 00:26:50,060
[SPEAKER_01]: By being mindful of your feelings?

324
00:26:50,721 --> 00:26:52,842
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those things can become a basis for your jhāna.

325
00:27:07,735 --> 00:27:18,278
[SPEAKER_00]: The one thing of mine that I can see that I did most of my things in my life was because of boredom.

326
00:27:18,878 --> 00:27:31,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Most things I did, yeah, getting involved in certain sensualities, you know, drinking, smoking, the whole business, watching movies, it's boredom.

327
00:27:33,322 --> 00:27:35,383
[SPEAKER_00]: I think most people get, yeah, it's the

328
00:27:38,101 --> 00:27:39,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Boredom and fear of boredom.

329
00:27:39,662 --> 00:27:43,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Fear of boredom, yeah.

330
00:27:44,263 --> 00:27:45,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Like genuine fear of boredom.

331
00:27:46,604 --> 00:27:52,167
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I'm a bit bored, but you actually, everybody, well, two generals, fear boredom.

332
00:27:54,788 --> 00:28:05,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when you do get extremely, extremely bored, extremely, extremely bored... Well, then you start losing, losing your sense of self.

333
00:28:07,783 --> 00:28:12,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, obviously, for somebody who is all about their sense of self, it's very frightening.

334
00:28:12,727 --> 00:28:14,828
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why boredom freaks people out.

335
00:28:15,368 --> 00:28:18,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can lose your sense of self, not in them.

336
00:28:18,571 --> 00:28:24,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not lose in terms of... fully extinguish it, as another hand.

337
00:28:25,335 --> 00:28:33,661
[SPEAKER_01]: But, basically, start feeling like you're not letting go of the sense of self, yet it's being taken away.

338
00:28:34,762 --> 00:28:36,183
[SPEAKER_01]: All right, but how is it taken away?

339
00:28:36,805 --> 00:28:41,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's taken away through boredom, basically, simply because boredom is unpleasant.

340
00:28:43,146 --> 00:28:51,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, you've been pursuing, everybody's been pursuing pleasures, habitually, and now they became dependent upon the pursuit of pleasures.

341
00:28:52,308 --> 00:29:05,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, when there are no pleasures, even if there is no particular pain, so it's just boredom, it's just a neutral feeling, but because you're so dependent upon pleasures, that neutral feeling starts to feel very unpleasant.

342
00:29:06,336 --> 00:29:09,577
[SPEAKER_01]: And not knowing the escape from unpleasant, you fear it even more.

343
00:29:09,717 --> 00:29:11,037
[SPEAKER_01]: You're affected by it even more.

344
00:29:11,757 --> 00:29:22,700
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, the whole thing of boredom just shows you that deep down, inside of your very sense of self, that you hold very dearly, you're not in control even of that.

345
00:29:22,720 --> 00:29:23,560
[SPEAKER_01]: And that freaks you out.

346
00:29:24,660 --> 00:29:27,561
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why in solitary confinement, people often go mad.

347
00:29:29,502 --> 00:29:31,882
[SPEAKER_01]: And when they come out, they're like, oh, he's a different person now.

348
00:29:32,062 --> 00:29:33,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he would be.

349
00:29:34,703 --> 00:29:35,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, you know,

350
00:29:36,167 --> 00:29:41,889
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be, for somebody with the right view, solitary confinement would probably result in arahantship.

351
00:29:43,450 --> 00:29:49,472
[SPEAKER_01]: But for those without the right view, even coming across nibbāna, it would freak them out, as they did in the suttas.

352
00:29:49,672 --> 00:29:54,113
[SPEAKER_01]: And they would understand the goal of dhamma when the Buddha... when the Buddha was pointing at this all.

353
00:29:54,133 --> 00:29:56,254
[SPEAKER_01]: They will annihilate us, they will kill us, they will destroy us.

354
00:29:56,914 --> 00:29:57,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

355
00:29:57,094 --> 00:29:59,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they... their minds, their attention is still very rooted.

356
00:30:00,275 --> 00:30:05,137
[SPEAKER_01]: They have completely gratuitously assumed sense of self, but that is real as such, and that's all they have at the time.

357
00:30:06,262 --> 00:30:20,568
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, if in that state of mind, they come across anything they will vaguely indicate they're not in control, there will be quite a good cause for anxiety, for a deep anxiety.

358
00:30:21,608 --> 00:30:26,890
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if you stay with that deep anxiety longer, in the case of prolonged boredom, you'll start freaking out.

359
00:30:27,410 --> 00:30:28,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you start looking for a way out.

360
00:30:29,811 --> 00:30:31,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Not everybody will freak out.

361
00:30:31,332 --> 00:30:32,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people will say,

362
00:30:33,458 --> 00:30:38,740
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to look if there is a... there is a freedom from this, because I'm not in control of my own very self.

363
00:30:40,180 --> 00:30:45,502
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I say I'm not in control, I don't mean, oh, I can do anything, which is something people end up doing after prolonged boredom.

364
00:30:46,203 --> 00:30:52,825
[SPEAKER_01]: But it means, basically, you have no say in the very foundations of your own being that you take to be yours.

365
00:30:52,965 --> 00:30:54,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, that's a big contradiction.

366
00:30:55,206 --> 00:30:59,067
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's mine, well, you would have a say, but you have no say.

367
00:30:59,828 --> 00:31:00,968
[SPEAKER_01]: So how can it be yours?

368
00:31:01,727 --> 00:31:04,108
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't know anything but to have it as yours.

369
00:31:05,709 --> 00:31:08,071
[SPEAKER_01]: But now everything is indicating you that it can't be yours.

370
00:31:09,251 --> 00:31:09,832
[SPEAKER_01]: So you have a choice.

371
00:31:10,312 --> 00:31:20,217
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're able to, you can then just turn a blind eye and run even faster from the fact that you have no say in it.

372
00:31:20,938 --> 00:31:30,583
[SPEAKER_01]: And always kind of be just a sort of enough, like, you know, basically you're drowning, but you're just enough space to have, like, a breath of air every now and then.

373
00:31:31,400 --> 00:31:32,781
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what most people's lives are.

374
00:31:33,261 --> 00:31:34,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Constant pursuit.

375
00:31:34,702 --> 00:31:36,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not...

376
00:31:36,323 --> 00:31:40,045
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, the types of pursuit are optional.

377
00:31:40,665 --> 00:31:43,907
[SPEAKER_01]: But the reasons for the pursuit for people out there are not optional.

378
00:31:44,687 --> 00:31:45,568
[SPEAKER_01]: They all have to do it.

379
00:31:46,128 --> 00:31:47,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't do it, you start sinking.

380
00:31:48,290 --> 00:31:48,990
[SPEAKER_01]: And where do you sink?

381
00:31:49,070 --> 00:31:54,373
[SPEAKER_01]: You sink in the... basically in the environment that shows you that

382
00:31:54,948 --> 00:31:58,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Things are indifferent to you.

383
00:31:58,269 --> 00:32:04,570
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no say in your own feelings, in your own intentions, in your own perceptions, yet you take them to be yours.

384
00:32:05,730 --> 00:32:20,533
[SPEAKER_01]: That feels basically like you're locked in a car, in a vehicle, that it's running down the hill, full speed, and actually the steering wheel is not working, maybe a little bit, but not in a sense that you can stop the car.

385
00:32:21,741 --> 00:32:25,544
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, all you can choose is where you're going to crash.

386
00:32:25,944 --> 00:32:31,168
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that, it's not ultimately up to you, because you might crash even before you reach where you want to crash at.

387
00:32:31,488 --> 00:32:35,671
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have you have a limited degree of control within the ultimate lack of control.

388
00:32:36,912 --> 00:32:37,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, right.

389
00:32:37,252 --> 00:32:44,398
[SPEAKER_00]: It sounds like suicide, you know, like the acts of suicide, you can... you don't really... Yeah, yeah, yeah.

390
00:32:44,718 --> 00:32:45,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what I said.

391
00:32:45,919 --> 00:32:49,081
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only kind of aim at the body and then wait for death to come.

392
00:32:51,312 --> 00:33:04,378
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's... that's... well, that's... that's basically... and even that, it's not... even that death that comes cannot be yours, any more than any other thing that you could do during your life can be yours.

393
00:33:09,860 --> 00:33:12,421
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one alternative is to keep running away from it.

394
00:33:12,441 --> 00:33:14,842
[SPEAKER_01]: The other alternative is to then recognize that this

395
00:33:16,049 --> 00:33:16,869
[SPEAKER_01]: This is not a good deal.

396
00:33:17,509 --> 00:33:19,310
[SPEAKER_01]: And the contradiction that your existence is.

397
00:33:19,410 --> 00:33:20,450
[SPEAKER_01]: So I'm here.

398
00:33:20,690 --> 00:33:21,270
[SPEAKER_01]: I am.

399
00:33:21,310 --> 00:33:22,330
[SPEAKER_01]: I feel things are mine.

400
00:33:22,710 --> 00:33:23,591
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'm not saying it.

401
00:33:24,311 --> 00:33:29,332
[SPEAKER_01]: So then I better make the effort and abandon that notion of I am and things are mine.

402
00:33:30,032 --> 00:33:35,533
[SPEAKER_01]: So then I won't be having to live this contradiction, which is actually unpleasant.

403
00:33:42,734 --> 00:33:45,475
[SPEAKER_00]: So be at the bottom of it.

404
00:33:46,099 --> 00:33:47,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Extreme boredom, you know.

405
00:33:50,422 --> 00:33:54,105
[SPEAKER_00]: There is nothing for you to do.

406
00:33:54,506 --> 00:33:55,787
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing for you to have.

407
00:33:56,707 --> 00:33:57,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there never was.

408
00:33:57,808 --> 00:33:59,009
[SPEAKER_00]: There never was, yeah.

409
00:33:59,029 --> 00:34:00,830
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's what boredom feels like.

410
00:34:03,052 --> 00:34:03,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

411
00:34:04,393 --> 00:34:06,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-activity.

412
00:34:07,375 --> 00:34:08,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Abiding in non-activity.

413
00:34:09,437 --> 00:34:10,578
[SPEAKER_01]: But they see, like, obviously,

414
00:34:11,163 --> 00:34:18,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Boredom, as a designation, it's already... it's already defined by Kutujanans, and it has... it has... it has a very negative connotation.

415
00:34:18,849 --> 00:34:19,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Boredom.

416
00:34:19,489 --> 00:34:19,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

417
00:34:20,990 --> 00:34:28,616
[SPEAKER_01]: And, see, it has a negative connotation, but it also implies quite some... quite some self-deception as well.

418
00:34:28,756 --> 00:34:29,457
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm bored.

419
00:34:29,637 --> 00:34:33,139
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of... it's kind of all right, but I don't like to be bored.

420
00:34:33,199 --> 00:34:40,144
[SPEAKER_01]: But, actually, boredom is quite serious, because you might kind of try to dismiss it by saying, oh, it's just boredom.

421
00:34:40,164 --> 00:34:40,905
[SPEAKER_01]: He's just bored.

422
00:34:41,696 --> 00:34:43,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Leave him bored a bit longer and then you'll see.

423
00:34:44,398 --> 00:34:45,559
[SPEAKER_01]: He'll start falling apart.

424
00:34:46,840 --> 00:34:48,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody would start falling apart.

425
00:34:48,302 --> 00:34:49,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, putujana would start falling apart.

426
00:34:50,003 --> 00:34:50,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

427
00:34:51,264 --> 00:34:55,668
[SPEAKER_01]: But more accurately, it's a neutral feeling that's obviously for putujana.

428
00:34:55,888 --> 00:34:56,669
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not known.

429
00:34:56,989 --> 00:34:57,830
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not understood.

430
00:34:58,290 --> 00:34:59,091
[SPEAKER_01]: It's being feared.

431
00:34:59,792 --> 00:35:00,272
[SPEAKER_01]: It's bored.

432
00:35:01,773 --> 00:35:02,374
[SPEAKER_01]: It's anxiety.

433
00:35:02,394 --> 00:35:03,635
[SPEAKER_01]: It's whatever else that comes out of it.

434
00:35:04,255 --> 00:35:06,037
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you actually recognize it as such,

435
00:35:06,789 --> 00:35:17,597
[SPEAKER_01]: So, recognizing in regard to, like, lowlier feelings of of sensual pleasure and sensual discomfort and so on, then you you actually know this is peaceful.

436
00:35:17,697 --> 00:35:18,558
[SPEAKER_01]: This is more sublime.

437
00:35:20,559 --> 00:35:21,240
[SPEAKER_01]: It's peace.

438
00:35:21,620 --> 00:35:22,201
[SPEAKER_01]: It's neutral.

439
00:35:24,983 --> 00:35:25,723
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's quite pleasant.

440
00:35:26,063 --> 00:35:29,546
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to find it pleasant, you need to elevate your mind to that level.

441
00:35:29,766 --> 00:35:31,307
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to train in regard to it.

442
00:35:31,868 --> 00:35:33,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind starts down in the gutter.

443
00:35:34,430 --> 00:35:34,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Always

444
00:35:35,865 --> 00:35:36,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Just a different extent.

445
00:35:37,366 --> 00:35:38,566
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you have to work your way up.

446
00:35:42,547 --> 00:35:43,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

447
00:35:44,068 --> 00:35:46,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

448
00:35:46,229 --> 00:36:00,573
[SPEAKER_00]: When someone is bored, in a negative sense, the mind starts to, you know, freak out, and you start craving, even craving those old senses, or more sensual things.

449
00:36:00,633 --> 00:36:05,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Because basically, that offers itself, as you spoke before, the sensual pleasure,

450
00:36:06,017 --> 00:36:08,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Kind of feels like a like a quick fix.

451
00:36:09,358 --> 00:36:23,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereby, not just that you get the pleasure of the sense that you're engaging in, but you also get the pleasure of just kind of avoiding this boring pain, this heavy anxious threat behind this boredom.

452
00:36:24,323 --> 00:36:30,205
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pleasure makes that disappear, seemingly, and provides you with the pleasure on top of it.

453
00:36:30,465 --> 00:36:31,686
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like a double whammy.

454
00:36:35,812 --> 00:36:40,137
[SPEAKER_00]: That if you don't go into sensual pleasures, you just crave for them.

455
00:36:40,998 --> 00:36:43,642
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just double the suffering right there, you know.

456
00:36:44,081 --> 00:36:44,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

457
00:36:44,341 --> 00:36:46,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you've got enough, you know, you're bored.

458
00:36:46,402 --> 00:36:48,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, absolutely.

459
00:36:48,483 --> 00:36:50,044
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have enough restraint.

460
00:36:50,484 --> 00:36:51,645
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you crave...

461
00:36:52,005 --> 00:37:02,851
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you suffer on account of boredom, because you're ignorant in regard to it, and then you suffer on account of uncontrollable craving that you are not, kind of, covering up through giving in to that craving.

462
00:37:03,211 --> 00:37:06,032
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have a double, double suffering that's being present.

463
00:37:06,313 --> 00:37:08,594
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly how it is, how it has to be in the beginning.

464
00:37:08,634 --> 00:37:12,576
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why vinaya, the sīla, has to come first.

465
00:37:13,433 --> 00:37:16,454
[SPEAKER_01]: The restraint, the precept, has to come first.

466
00:37:16,994 --> 00:37:19,995
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a dhamma, but there wouldn't be any dhamma without it.

467
00:37:20,255 --> 00:37:24,496
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it has to come first, and then you can start building within that.

468
00:37:25,156 --> 00:37:27,616
[SPEAKER_00]: From there, you can see craving equals suffering.

469
00:37:27,636 --> 00:37:27,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

470
00:37:28,176 --> 00:37:29,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving equals suffering.

471
00:37:30,397 --> 00:37:34,878
[SPEAKER_01]: You were craving for this body to be absent, to be not present.

472
00:37:35,545 --> 00:37:37,486
[SPEAKER_01]: You are craving for pleasure to be present.

473
00:37:37,626 --> 00:37:41,167
[SPEAKER_01]: You have craving on both levels, you have suffering on both levels.

474
00:37:41,547 --> 00:37:42,968
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't suffer, actually.

475
00:37:43,348 --> 00:37:47,710
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't suffer because you're not pursuing your desires.

476
00:37:47,750 --> 00:37:52,732
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that non-pursuit of desires shows you the craving behind them, which is why you suffer.

477
00:37:54,152 --> 00:38:02,596
[SPEAKER_01]: But it takes time to discern that, because craving, you give in, you go after the desire, and pain disappears.

478
00:38:02,656 --> 00:38:03,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Or so it seems.

479
00:38:07,053 --> 00:38:09,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the pain disappears because you stop craving.

480
00:38:10,594 --> 00:38:13,894
[SPEAKER_01]: And you stop craving because you have the assumption that you satisfied your desire.

481
00:38:14,875 --> 00:38:18,575
[SPEAKER_01]: But that assumption has nothing to do with the actual satisfaction of your senses or not.

482
00:38:18,635 --> 00:38:19,415
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just an assumption.

483
00:38:19,435 --> 00:38:21,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why, next time, you probably need more of it.

484
00:38:22,276 --> 00:38:23,356
[SPEAKER_01]: And more of it.

485
00:38:23,376 --> 00:38:24,296
[SPEAKER_01]: And harder scratch.

486
00:38:24,817 --> 00:38:25,617
[SPEAKER_01]: And harder push.

487
00:38:25,677 --> 00:38:26,357
[SPEAKER_01]: And so on.

488
00:38:26,477 --> 00:38:27,517
[SPEAKER_01]: And for what?

489
00:38:27,557 --> 00:38:29,697
[SPEAKER_01]: For the same assumption of, OK, now I'm fine.

490
00:38:31,018 --> 00:38:32,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Body might feel, eh...

491
00:38:33,149 --> 00:38:34,449
[SPEAKER_01]: At ease, temporarily.

492
00:38:34,829 --> 00:38:37,690
[SPEAKER_01]: So craving disappears, and then suffering disappears.

493
00:38:37,730 --> 00:38:40,450
[SPEAKER_01]: But you think suffering disappeared because you satisfied your desire.

494
00:38:41,110 --> 00:38:47,051
[SPEAKER_01]: But you didn't, because when craving comes back, and it does always comes back, because it's not distinguished, suffering appears.

495
00:38:47,532 --> 00:38:50,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And then again, you don't blame the craving for that suffering.

496
00:38:50,392 --> 00:38:53,373
[SPEAKER_01]: You blame, oh, it's a new desire that I need to satisfy.

497
00:38:54,653 --> 00:38:58,974
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's actually, if there is a presence of craving, means suffering is present.

498
00:39:00,154 --> 00:39:01,254
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot suffer

499
00:39:01,912 --> 00:39:20,483
[SPEAKER_01]: On any other account, except one single account, presence of craving, in regard to the most extreme circumstances, the most extreme discomfort situations, experiences, if there is suffering present, it's not because of them, it's because of the presence of craving in regard to those circumstances.

500
00:39:23,165 --> 00:39:29,549
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's... that's why the people... restraint for people means pain, and they think

501
00:39:31,446 --> 00:39:35,068
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm hurting now because I'm holding myself back.

502
00:39:35,549 --> 00:39:37,330
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm hurting because I'm restrained.

503
00:39:38,531 --> 00:39:38,711
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

504
00:39:39,352 --> 00:39:50,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Restraint is not... basically, restraint is a side thing whereby if you engage in restraint, what is revealed is that craving is suffering.

505
00:39:52,081 --> 00:39:52,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

506
00:39:53,462 --> 00:39:56,925
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to see that, you have to stop, you have to practice restraint sufficiently

507
00:39:58,150 --> 00:40:00,892
[SPEAKER_01]: For you to know that you should stop blaming for pain.

508
00:40:02,434 --> 00:40:04,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop assuming that that is the reason for your pain.

509
00:40:04,696 --> 00:40:05,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

510
00:40:07,118 --> 00:40:12,763
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sensuality in itself is inseparable from craving.

511
00:40:12,983 --> 00:40:16,306
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the perceptions of sensuality are inseparable from craving.

512
00:40:16,366 --> 00:40:17,427
[SPEAKER_01]: They imply craving.

513
00:40:18,068 --> 00:40:24,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you perceiving sensual desire means craving is present, means suffering is present.

514
00:40:25,595 --> 00:40:31,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you restraining yourself in regard to the pursuit of the sensual desire, pain arises immediately.

515
00:40:31,679 --> 00:40:33,140
[SPEAKER_01]: You're saying no to yourself.

516
00:40:34,201 --> 00:40:35,982
[SPEAKER_01]: But that pain is not because you said no.

517
00:40:36,483 --> 00:40:43,267
[SPEAKER_01]: That pain is because saying no to yourself revealed the underlying setup of presence of craving is very painful.

518
00:40:44,368 --> 00:40:46,029
[SPEAKER_01]: And that pain, how can you deal with it?

519
00:40:46,589 --> 00:40:52,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Either by extinguishing craving, which is for most people, well, impossible.

520
00:40:52,454 --> 00:40:54,375
[SPEAKER_01]: They simply don't know how

521
00:40:55,483 --> 00:40:59,344
[SPEAKER_01]: You have not been instructed, have not heard a dhamma, you have no concept of it.

522
00:40:59,384 --> 00:41:07,347
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way... like, it's not really escape, but the only way they could approximate the escape would be giving in.

523
00:41:08,447 --> 00:41:10,608
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, again, happens exactly what I said before.

524
00:41:11,088 --> 00:41:18,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, not just that you kind of obscured the pain that has arisen, but now you also got the pleasure of the senses.

525
00:41:19,451 --> 00:41:20,811
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you kind of feel, oh, this is good.

526
00:41:20,992 --> 00:41:23,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I avoided the pain and I got pleasure as a result of it.

527
00:41:23,792 --> 00:41:25,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's... you can't ask for more.

528
00:41:25,998 --> 00:41:35,368
[SPEAKER_01]: But the problem is, the underlying setup of presence of craving, presence of suffering, not just that it hasn't been affected or resolved, it just has been fortified more.

529
00:41:36,029 --> 00:41:43,637
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the more you pursue desires, the more you'll crave, which means the more... the more the craving is present, the more the suffering is present.

530
00:41:44,260 --> 00:41:46,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence the simile of scratching the itch.

531
00:41:46,381 --> 00:41:48,743
[SPEAKER_01]: You get that relief temporarily while you're scratching it.

532
00:41:49,143 --> 00:41:51,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Overall, you're making it much, much worse.

533
00:41:51,684 --> 00:42:03,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Next time, it will scratch harder, and harder, and harder, until eventually, you're just like a mangy doll that's not even aware of it, but it's perpetually, perpetually just scratching and ripping its skin open.

534
00:42:04,032 --> 00:42:05,213
[SPEAKER_01]: And it doesn't even know it anymore.

535
00:42:05,233 --> 00:42:07,594
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just doing it as a habit, just to scratch, just...

536
00:42:08,154 --> 00:42:10,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind is elsewhere, and you're just ripping it, just scratching it.

537
00:42:11,981 --> 00:42:15,503
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's, you know, quite, quite, quite only human existence.

538
00:42:26,269 --> 00:42:26,930
[SPEAKER_00]: It's backboarding.

539
00:42:27,310 --> 00:42:27,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Uh-huh.

540
00:42:28,410 --> 00:42:29,611
[SPEAKER_00]: It feels like you're done.

541
00:42:30,692 --> 00:42:30,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

542
00:42:30,992 --> 00:42:32,653
[SPEAKER_00]: When there's no need to resist happening.

543
00:42:35,575 --> 00:42:38,036
[SPEAKER_01]: You feel like, it feels like you're being annihilated.

544
00:42:39,277 --> 00:42:39,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Gradually.

545
00:42:43,144 --> 00:42:44,864
[SPEAKER_00]: That can be quite a pleasant thing, isn't it?

546
00:42:44,964 --> 00:42:45,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you start having...

547
00:42:45,864 --> 00:42:52,486
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not holding on to the contradiction of I am, while that I am is being annihilated, that's a discrepancy.

548
00:42:54,606 --> 00:43:02,187
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you avoid, you overcome that discrepancy, well, by basically not holding to I am anymore.

549
00:43:06,028 --> 00:43:10,489
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then there won't be any discrepancy splitting, hurting you, annihilating you.

550
00:43:12,912 --> 00:43:13,553
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite pleasant.

551
00:43:14,073 --> 00:43:14,493
[SPEAKER_01]: We're here.

552
00:43:15,695 --> 00:43:16,856
[SPEAKER_01]: It's deliberation.

553
00:43:22,000 --> 00:43:25,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, as in a monastic life.

554
00:43:26,124 --> 00:43:28,366
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me just get from practice to practice.

555
00:43:29,027 --> 00:43:29,768
[SPEAKER_00]: They're calling me.

556
00:43:44,112 --> 00:43:45,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Nice.

557
00:43:45,132 --> 00:43:52,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Go on, sorry.

558
00:43:52,416 --> 00:44:05,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, in monastic life, you can't quite... well, I came across the plot anyway... that extreme boredom, and then just constant thoughts of... you're dying, and then ideas of how to survive.

559
00:44:06,942 --> 00:44:09,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Try and, you know, your mind goes back to the past.

560
00:44:10,123 --> 00:44:11,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Try to rekindle yourself.

561
00:44:14,772 --> 00:44:16,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

562
00:44:16,073 --> 00:44:16,674
[SPEAKER_00]: What about this?

563
00:44:16,714 --> 00:44:18,436
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, don't lose yourself.

564
00:44:19,798 --> 00:44:22,761
[SPEAKER_00]: That desperate... Yeah, there's aspiration.

565
00:44:22,781 --> 00:44:24,643
[SPEAKER_00]: ...to survive for that self.

566
00:44:24,703 --> 00:44:28,427
[SPEAKER_01]: It would revolve around things, around the memories, around the thoughts.

567
00:44:28,928 --> 00:44:31,811
[SPEAKER_01]: They were dear, they were significant to your sense of self.

568
00:44:39,953 --> 00:44:46,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I had to be bored, I'm sure that would come up again, that desperate attempt to survive.

569
00:44:46,337 --> 00:44:51,321
[SPEAKER_00]: But being able to watch, being able to be...

570
00:44:52,381 --> 00:44:55,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Being able to not act out of it.

571
00:44:56,344 --> 00:44:58,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, to not act out of fear of boredom.

572
00:45:00,207 --> 00:45:01,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Not act out of fear, full stop.

573
00:45:03,809 --> 00:45:08,012
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to allow that boredom and that fear to arise.

574
00:45:08,152 --> 00:45:08,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

575
00:45:09,352 --> 00:45:11,293
[SPEAKER_00]: And just sit through it, kind of thing.

576
00:45:11,313 --> 00:45:11,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Watch it.

577
00:45:12,113 --> 00:45:12,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

578
00:45:13,413 --> 00:45:22,516
[SPEAKER_01]: If then something happens that had nothing to do with your intention to run away from it, but if something happens...

579
00:45:22,536 --> 00:45:24,777
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just watching a mosquito is trying to enter through the curtain.

580
00:45:26,798 --> 00:45:27,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so...

581
00:45:27,638 --> 00:45:38,922
[SPEAKER_01]: If something happens while you're being bored, but that's not the reason... if that which happens is not the result of you trying to run away, trying to, you know, cover up the boredom,

582
00:45:39,626 --> 00:45:40,086
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine.

583
00:45:41,447 --> 00:45:43,888
[SPEAKER_01]: What won't be fine is hoping for something to happen.

584
00:45:45,409 --> 00:45:50,991
[SPEAKER_01]: What won't be fine is projecting that which happened, because you must be bored.

585
00:45:55,573 --> 00:45:59,395
[SPEAKER_01]: But that all comes through training and boredom.

586
00:46:00,596 --> 00:46:05,478
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be able to start deciding it through non-distraction,

587
00:46:06,396 --> 00:46:07,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Through restraint.

588
00:46:07,497 --> 00:46:08,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, restraint.

589
00:46:08,317 --> 00:46:08,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

590
00:46:08,658 --> 00:46:08,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

591
00:46:09,018 --> 00:46:09,898
[SPEAKER_01]: And then it will come.

592
00:46:09,938 --> 00:46:17,784
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more it comes, the more efficient it will become, the more proficient, the more it is, the more it is, better you see it, clearly you see it.

593
00:46:19,425 --> 00:46:21,726
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, oh, look, this is training in peace.

594
00:46:25,308 --> 00:46:26,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Could someone else... Yeah.

595
00:46:28,631 --> 00:46:30,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Someone who's not doing anything, just sitting around.

596
00:46:31,753 --> 00:46:32,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter.

597
00:46:33,052 --> 00:46:36,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, eh... externally, they might be just sitting around.

598
00:46:36,734 --> 00:46:38,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Internally, they're probably doing a lot of wrong things.

599
00:46:39,435 --> 00:46:39,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

600
00:46:39,675 --> 00:46:42,477
[SPEAKER_01]: For wrong reasons.

601
00:46:48,720 --> 00:46:48,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

602
00:46:48,840 --> 00:46:51,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind is better off in this direction.

603
00:46:52,502 --> 00:46:56,504
[SPEAKER_01]: And even when you say people just sit around, they're not doing anything, that tends to them not doing work.

604
00:46:56,964 --> 00:46:57,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

605
00:46:57,384 --> 00:47:02,727
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're sitting around means doing things they want to do, which means they're not necessarily bored, just be sitting around.

606
00:47:03,973 --> 00:47:05,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, nobody likes boredom.

607
00:47:05,373 --> 00:47:07,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Arjuna likes it, because it's unboredom.

608
00:47:08,454 --> 00:47:12,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Sotāpanna appreciates it, because it can make him into an Arjuna.

609
00:47:12,054 --> 00:47:14,615
[SPEAKER_01]: But Puṭṭhārjuna fears it.

610
00:47:16,495 --> 00:47:17,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it can kill him, yeah.

611
00:47:18,175 --> 00:47:26,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, because he doesn't know how to let go of the insense itself, and that creates a rather unbearable discrepancy.

612
00:47:26,937 --> 00:47:32,418
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm holding to this, which now, it's kind of obvious, cannot be mine, yet I cannot not hold on to it.

613
00:47:34,126 --> 00:47:38,829
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it really feels like you're being, kind of, torched from inside.

614
00:47:40,910 --> 00:47:43,911
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, people lose their minds, often, on account of it.

615
00:47:44,031 --> 00:47:44,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Just get depressed.

616
00:47:45,552 --> 00:47:47,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Or get really depressed, yeah.

617
00:47:47,533 --> 00:47:48,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Catatonic.

618
00:47:49,334 --> 00:47:49,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

619
00:47:55,577 --> 00:47:57,959
[SPEAKER_00]: It always seemed like something was wrong, isn't it?

620
00:47:58,019 --> 00:47:59,920
[SPEAKER_00]: That boredom, something's wrong.

621
00:48:01,092 --> 00:48:04,475
[SPEAKER_01]: That's, again, that's just the view coming from the point of view of sense itself.

622
00:48:04,695 --> 00:48:05,536
[SPEAKER_00]: It's what it's used to.

623
00:48:06,076 --> 00:48:06,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

624
00:48:06,917 --> 00:48:13,523
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start undoing that, all those kind of collateral views will also start fading.

